course: biology
lesson: 227
title: The vet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A vet is a doctor for animals, but unlike a children's doctor, a vet must know about dogs, cats, hamsters, horses, cows and snakes, all with different bodies, and none of the patients can say where it hurts.
explain: So a vet looks, listens and measures: the stethoscope tells about the heart and lungs, the thermometer about fever, the scales about weight, and an X-ray shows what is inside, like the sock a greedy dog swallowed.
explain: At the yearly check-up a dog gets an injection, a vaccine that teaches its body to fight dangerous illnesses before it ever meets them.
explain: After an operation a cat may come home wearing a big cone round its neck, which looks funny but stops it licking open its stitches.
explain: Farm vets drive out to check cows and horses, and when a pet is very old and suffering, a vet can help it die peacefully, which is sad but can be the kindest thing.
fact: Vaccines given by injection protect pets by helping their bodies learn to fight illnesses.
fact: Vets use a stethoscope, just like doctors, to listen to an animal's heart and lungs.
fact: Vets often take a pet's temperature with a thermometer under the tail, because it gives an accurate reading.
fact: Weight tells a lot about health; losing weight can be the first sign of illness, so even a hamster is weighed on small scales.
fact: Horses' teeth keep growing and wearing down, and sharp edges can make eating painful, so vets or horse dentists file them smooth with special tools.
fact: Dogs sometimes swallow odd things like socks and toys; an X-ray can show them, and some objects block the gut and need an operation.
fact: A cone round a pet's neck after an operation stops it licking its stitches, because licking can open the wound.
fact: A brown crust on a pet's teeth is tartar; food and germs build up on teeth, make the gums sore and can make teeth fall out.
fact: Farm vets visit farms to check herds of cows, sheep and pigs.
fact: When a pet is very old or ill and suffering, a vet can help it die peacefully; this is called putting to sleep.
fact: Vets study for about five years or more at university and learn about many different kinds of animal.
fact: A vet checks a pet's eyes, ears, teeth, skin and coat during a check-up, because many illnesses show there first.
exercise: Why does a dog get an injection at the vet each year? | A vaccine helps its body learn to fight illnesses | The injection makes it grow bigger and stronger | The injection gives it food for the year | The injection makes it sleep through the night without barking
exercise: What does a vet use to listen to a cat's heart and lungs? | A stethoscope | A thermometer | A microscope | A pair of scales
exercise: Where do vets often take a dog's temperature? | Under the tail, for an accurate reading | Inside the ear, using a little spoon | By touching the nose only | By looking very closely into its eyes
exercise: Why does a vet weigh a tiny hamster? | Losing weight can be the first sign of illness | To see if it is ready to be sold | To check how fast it can run on its wheel | Only to know which size of cage to use
exercise: Why does a vet file a horse's teeth? | Sharp edges make eating painful | To make the horse look whiter | To stop the teeth growing at all | To help the horse neigh louder
exercise: What can an X-ray show inside a dog? | A swallowed sock blocking its tummy | What the dog is thinking about right now | The dog's favourite food | How old the dog's owner is
exercise: Why might a dog that swallowed a sock need an operation? | The sock can block the gut | Socks are poisonous to dogs | The sock will grow inside the dog | The dog will start to smell of feet
exercise: Why does a cat wear a cone after an operation? | To stop it licking open its stitches | To help it hear better while it is ill | To keep its head warm | To stop it eating too much while it gets better
exercise: What is the brown crust on an old dog's teeth? | Tartar, built up from food and germs | Chocolate stuck to the teeth | A sign that the teeth are very strong | Dirt from digging in the garden for bones
exercise: What can happen if tartar is left on a dog's teeth? | Sore gums, and teeth can fall out | The teeth become much stronger | The dog's breath smells of mint | Nothing ever happens to the teeth
exercise: What does a farm vet do? | Visits farms to check cows and other animals | Only looks after pet cats in town | Grows crops for the animals | Drives the farm's tractor in the fields at harvest time
exercise: What can a vet do when a pet is very old, ill and suffering? | Help it die peacefully, called putting to sleep | Make it young again with a special medicine | Send it to live on a farm for ever | Nothing, vets never help old pets
exercise: A girl wants to become a vet. What will she need to do? | Study for years and learn about many animals | Just love animals, no study needed | Learn all about only one kind of animal | Train for a few weeks at a pet shop
exercise: Why does a vet look at a pet's ears, eyes and skin at a check-up? | Many illnesses show there first | To choose the pet's new name | To decide what colour collar to buy | To see if the pet is happy with its owner
exercise: How is a vet's job different from a children's doctor's? | Vets treat many species and patients cannot talk | Vets only ever treat sick goldfish | Vets do not need to study | Vets only work at night, when the animals are asleep
test: «why's the vet sticking a needle in my dog, that's mean» What is the reason? | It is a vaccine that protects him from illnesses | It is to punish him for being bad | It is to make him sleep for ever | It is to make his fur grow back thicker and shinier
test: In a cartoon a dog swallows a whole alarm clock and you can hear it ticking. What is true about real dogs? | They do swallow odd things, and an X-ray can show them | They can swallow anything at all with no harm | Things they swallow always disappear | Only cartoon dogs ever swallow objects
test: «cat's got a massive lampshade on her head lol, take it off, she hates it» What is right? | Leave it on; it stops her licking open her stitches | Take it off, it is only a joke from the vet | Take it off, because cones are bad for cats' ears | Leave it on, it makes her hear better
test: «my hamster's lost 10 grams, he's fine innit, he's just on a diet» What is wise? | Weight loss can be the first sign of illness; see a vet | Hamsters often diet on purpose | Weight never matters for small pets like hamsters | Feed him chocolate to put the weight back on quickly
test: Count them: a vet checks 3 dogs and 2 cats. How many ears does she look into? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 8
test: «old dog's breath stinks and his teeth are all brown» What is most likely? | Tartar is building up and his gums may be sore | He has been eating chocolate | Brown teeth mean healthy teeth | His teeth are changing colour with the seasons
test: What would happen if a horse's sharp tooth edges were never filed? | Eating could become painful | Its teeth would stop growing | It would eat faster than ever | Nothing, horses have no teeth
test: Count them: a farm vet checks 6 cows. How many legs does she check for limping? | 24 | 12 | 6 | 30
test: «the vet says Grandad's old cat is suffering and can be put to sleep» What does this mean? | The vet can help the cat die peacefully | The cat will sleep for a week and wake up | The cat will be given a sleeping pill each night | The vet will take the cat to live on a farm
test: «I wanna be a vet, how hard can it be, I love my dog» What is right? | Vets study for years and learn about many species | Loving dogs is all you need | You can start next week without any study at all | Vets only need to learn about dogs
test: In a TV show a toy doctor heals a toy by just giving it a hug. What does a real vet do first? | Looks, listens and measures to find the problem | Gives every animal a hug and sends it home | Asks the animal where it hurts | Guesses and gives the same medicine to every animal
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the injection administered to a dog at its annual examination. | The vaccine enables the body to acquire resistance against specified diseases | The injection supplies nourishment sufficient for a period of one year | The injection promotes an increase in the size of the animal | The injection ensures uninterrupted sleep during the night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the protective cone fitted to a cat following surgery? | It prevents the animal from licking its sutures and thereby reopening the wound | It improves the hearing of the animal during its recovery | It maintains the temperature of the head during convalescence | It restricts the quantity of food consumed by the animal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the brown deposit observed upon the teeth of an elderly dog. | It is tartar, which may cause inflammation of the gums and the loss of teeth | It is a residue of chocolate, which is harmless to the animal | It is an indication of exceptional dental strength | It is soil acquired during digging and is of no consequence whatsoever to the animal
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a veterinary surgeon measures the weight of a small animal such as a hamster. | A loss of weight may constitute the earliest indication of illness | The weight determines the price at which the animal may be sold | The weight determines the speed at which the animal can run | The weight is recorded solely for the selection of a cage of suitable dimensions
